{"format":"hologram.registry.artifact/v1","id":"mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/rust","fetched":"2026-09-25T12:12:08.381Z","source":{"name":"Microsoft Artifact Registry","api":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/api/v1/catalog/azurelinux/base/rust/details?reg=mar","page":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/en-us/product/azurelinux/base/rust/about"},"readme":"## About\n\nThis repository contains the official Rust image for Azure Linux.\n\n### What is Azure Linux?\n\nAzure Linux is a Linux distribution for Microsoft’s cloud infrastructure and edge products and services. Azure Linux is designed to provide a consistent platform for these devices and services and will enhance Microsoft’s ability to stay current on Linux updates.  \n\nTo learn more about Azure Linux, refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n### What is Rust?\n\nRust is a systems programming language sponsored by Mozilla Research. It is designed to be a \"safe, concurrent, practical language\", supporting functional and imperative-procedural paradigms. Rust is syntactically similar to C++, but is designed for better memory safety while maintaining performance.\n\nSee: [Rust (programming language)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rust_%28programming_language%29)\n\n## Featured tags\n\n- `1`\n\nThe featured tags are multi-architecture tags (i.e., `x86-64` and `aarch64`) that refer to the latest version of the image. Additional tags that are specific to rust version, Azure Linux build, or architecture can be found in the Tags section.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Running a simple Rust app\n\n`1.` Create a simple Dockerfile to generate a new image that includes the necessary content.\n\n    FROM mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/rust:1\n\n    WORKDIR /usr/src/app\n\n    # Copy your rust app source code files to the work directory.\n    COPY my-rust-app/ .\n\n    # Run cargo install to build the rust app.\n    RUN cargo install --path .\n\n    CMD [ \"myapp\" ]\n\n`2.` Build and run your container image.\n\n    docker build -t rust-app-image .\n    docker run -it --rm --name running-rust-app rust-app-image\n\nThe default container startup command is `bash`.\n\n### Compile your app inside the docker container\n\nThere may be occasions where it is not appropriate to run your app inside a container. To compile, but not run your app inside the Docker instance, you can write something like:\n\n    docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/usr/src/myapp -w /usr/src/myapp mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/rust:1 cargo build –release\n\nThis will add your current directory as a volume to the container, set the working directory to the volume, and run the command `cargo build --release`. This tells Cargo (Rust's build system) to compile the crate in *myapp* and output the executable to *target/release/myapp*.\n\n### Launch Azure Linux shell\n\nThe Rust image ships with a bash shell. The following command will launch the shell:\n\n    docker run -it --rm mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/rust:1\n\n## Support\n\nAzure Linux container images are supported only for internal Microsoft use. For more information please see our [Support Statement](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/-/SUPPORT.md).\n\n## License\n\nThe Azure Linux distribution is provided under the [MIT license](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/-/LICENSE). Individual packages and components are governed by their own licenses included in the package sources available via srpm on [packages.microsoft.com](https://packages.microsoft.com/azurelinux/) or via our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n- Legal Notice: [Container License Information](https://aka.ms/mcr/osslegalnotice)","readmeBase":null,"publisher":{"name":"Microsoft","url":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/","official":true,"verified":true},"registered":null,"updated":"2026-09-12T02:12:08.0481672+00:00","categories":[],"keywords":[],"license":"MIT","links":[],"maintainers":[],"stats":{"pulls":null,"pullsText":"30K+","stars":nu